{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And Abraham drew near and said, Will You even sweep away the righteous with the wicked?</b> The anger of the Holy One, blessed be He, is His attribute of justice, and Abraham thought that it would sweep away the righteous with the wicked. He did not know the thoughts of YHWH, which He planned concerning them in His mercy, as I have explained. Therefore he said that it is fitting and good that He bear with the entire place for the sake of the fifty righteous. But it is impossible even according to the attribute of justice to put to death the righteous with the wicked, for if so the righteous would be like the wicked, and they would say, \"It is vain to serve Elohim\" (Malachi 3:14). How much more so according to the attribute of mercy — for He is the Judge of all the earth and He performs justice, as in the matter of \"And YHWH of hosts is exalted through justice\" (Isaiah 5:16), and as we say (Berakhot 12), \"the King of judgment.\" And this is the meaning of the doubling: \"Far be it from You.\" And the Holy One, blessed be He, acknowledged that He would bear with the entire place for their sake, for according to the attribute of mercy He conducts Himself with them. And what will inform you of the entire matter is that \"וַיֹּאמֶר ה'\" (and YHWH said) is written with the letters yod-he, while everywhere that Abraham mentioned the Name he used alef-dalet (i.e., Adonai). And this is clarified:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "Ramban notes a precise alternation: the narrator's references to the divine speech use the Tetragrammaton (indicated by the Hebrew letters יו\"ד ה\"א), while Abraham's direct addresses use אדני (indicated by אלפ\"א דל\"ת)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:31:32.476364759Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}